/E (1. Definition of the Problem)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
316 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 162 0 R
/E (The problem we are dealing with here is figuring out the shortest driving distance between two cities out of a total of 500 cities that are all connected with distances. Think of it like a GPS trying to tell you the fastest way to get from point A to point B. The system we built needs to take a starting city and a destination, then calculate the best possible route based on the distances between cities. After that, it should show both the full path and the total distance. We also had to measure how long each algorithm takes to finish so we could compare their performance. One important thing is that all the distances are non-negative, which makes sense for real-world maps - you cannot have a negative distance between two places.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
317 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 162 0 R
/E (This kind of problem shows up everywhere in real life. GPS apps like Google Maps or Waze use it all the time. Ride-sharing services like Uber and Careem rely on it too. Delivery companies like Amazon or FedEx need it for planning their routes. Even emergency services like ambulances and fire trucks depend on finding the quickest path. Network routing protocols also work similarly when they are trying to find the best way to send data across the internet.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
