374 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 164 0 R
/E (Let's start with Dijkstra. This one works by gradually exploring outward from the starting point. It always picks the closest city that hasn't been processed yet and checks its neighbors. By using a priority queue, it makes sure it's always looking at the most promising option next. It's really reliable as long as none of the distances are negative, which is perfect for GPS navigation.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
375 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 164 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
376 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 164 0 R
/E (Floyd-Warshall takes a completely different approach. Instead of finding just one route, it figures out the shortest path between every possible pair of cities all at once using dynamic programming. That sounds useful, but the problem is it's extremely slow when you have a lot of cities. For 500 cities, it needs about 125 million operations, which is way too much for real-time use.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [22]
>>
endobj
