1500 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 188 0 R
/E (After running all these tests and analyzing both the theoretical and experimental results, we've learned quite a bit about how these four shortest-path algorithms compare when you're dealing with a network of up to 500 cities.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1501 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 188 0 R
/E (The main thing we found is that Dijkstra's algorithm is the best choice for this particular problem. It was the fastest in our experiments, it always finds the correct shortest path, and it's already widely used in real-world GPS applications. That makes sense because it was designed exactly for this kind of scenario.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1502 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 188 0 R
/E (A* came in second place. It can be very fast, but that speed really depends on having a good heuristic. In our case, since we didn't have actual city coordinates to work with, the heuristic always returned zero, which made A* behave almost exactly like Dijkstra. So it's good, but not necessarily better than Dijkstra without additional data.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1503 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 188 0 R
/E (Bellman-Ford was slower, but it does have one advantage: it can handle negative weights. The problem is that negative distances don't really exist between cities, so that advantage wasn't useful for us. Given how much slower it was, we can't really recommend it for GPS navigation.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1504 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 188 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1505 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 188 0 R
/E (So to wrap it up, if you're building a GPS navigation system for 500 cities, go with Dijkstra. It's reliable, fast, and does exactly what you need without any unnecessary complications.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
